  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` is a coverage exception. The justification is in fenced commentary, as the built-in requires, and it matches the current source.\n\n- **Guard behavior:** `read` always returns `SourceError::Invalid(\"cached parser must not refetch\")`. That is an error guard, as the commentary says.\n- **Cached snapshots:** `impl[CachedParse/SourceProvider]/fn[parse]` is overridden. It returns a fixed in-memory snapshot through `parse_snapshot`, so the fixture supplies cached snapshots by overriding parse.\n- **No refetch:** The commentary says structural search must use those snapshots without refetching. A call to `read` would fail the test, so the line is not expected to run. I did not read the production search code or the test's assertions, so this rests on the guard's error and the commentary.\n- **Other providers:** Other providers in the same file implement and exercise `read`: `impl[Memory/SourceProvider]/fn[read]`, `impl[Primitives/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` and `impl[FailingProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]`. Tests such as `defaults_use_provider_primitives_and_keep_one_parse_snapshot` and `local_provider_scopes_paths_and_rejects_resources_outside_its_root` call `read`. That supports the claim that primitive reads are tested elsewhere.\n\nThe reason is substantive and not merely difficulty. The citation sits on an explicit function with a body, which the built-in permits. It does not duplicate an `{impl}` or `{test}` reference.",
